\chapter{Architecture and the Feature Catalogue}
\label{ch:architecture}

\section{What Architecture Means for a Single Header}

Most libraries have an architecture in the ordinary sense: a directory tree, a
separation between public headers and implementation files, a build system
that produces one or more artefacts. \DSLtk{} has none of that. It is one
file of 12,024 lines and a set of conventions. Its architecture is therefore
not a layout but a \emph{structure of concepts} -- a small number of
primitives, a uniform way of extending them, and a layering that keeps the
parts from interfering with one another.

This chapter makes that structure explicit. It maps the physical regions of
\code{DSLtk.hpp}, distinguishes the two kinds of feature the library
defines, walks the layering from core value types up to graphs and terms,
explains the convention that almost every public entry point is a
namespace-scope free function, and closes with a catalogue of all
twenty-six feature tags and the free types that accompany them. The D port's
different composition mechanism is described at the end, because it is the
clearest way to see what the C++ design is actually doing.

