Second, the standalone library region is by far the largest block of
\DSLtk{}'s own code, and it sits \emph{after} the feature tags that refer to
it. The ordering is not accidental: the feature tags near the top of the
namespace are the ones a DSL author interacts with, while the machinery they
name is defined further down.

\section{Two Kinds of Feature}

The single most important structural fact about \DSLtk{} is that its
twenty-six feature tags are not all the same kind of thing. Seven of them
inject members into the deriving class. Nineteen are \emph{markers}: their
\code{Mixin} is an empty struct, and naming them in a feature list changes
nothing about the class you derive.

Listing~\ref{lst:ch03-kinds} shows the two shapes side by side. On the left
is \code{dsl::Pipeline}, whose mixin contributes a static \code{wrap}
template. On the right is \code{dsl::PatternMatch}, whose mixin is empty.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={An injecting feature (left) and a marker feature (right), both from the shipped header.},label={lst:ch03-kinds}]
struct Pipeline
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    template <typename T>
    static constexpr T
    wrap (T &&v)
    {
      return std::forward<T> (v);
    }
  };
};

struct PatternMatch
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    // Feature presence marker; no additional methods needed since
    // dsl::match/when/otherwise are free functions in the dsl:: namespace.
  };
};
\end{lstlisting}

The distinction is easy to miss because both kinds have exactly the same
outer shape, and because the header's documentation comments sometimes
suggest that a marker does more than it does. The \code{PatternMatch}
comment above the tag says the feature \emph{``Exposes dsl::match /
dsl::when / dsl::otherwise as static methods on the derived class for
convenience''}, but the mixin body contains only a comment saying the
opposite. In the shipped code, a DSL that names \code{dsl::PatternMatch} does
\emph{not} acquire a \code{match} member; \code{dsl::match} remains a
namespace-scope free function and must be called as such. The same is true
of every other marker.

This is not a defect so much as a design position, and it is worth stating
plainly: in \DSLtk{}, a feature tag is a \emph{namesake}. Naming
\code{dsl::Visitors} in your feature list tells a reader -- and a tool --
that your DSL is a visitor-bearing DSL, and it lets the \code{HasFeature}
concept answer questions about it. It does not, by itself, give you
anything to call.

\subsection{The Seven Injecting Features}

These are the tags whose \code{Mixin} contributes something.

\begin{itemize}
\item \code{dsl::Pipeline} (line 6109) contributes a static
      \code{wrap(T&&)} template that lifts a value into a chain of
      transformations.
\item \code{dsl::Operators} (line 6143) contributes a static
      \code{make_pred(F&&)} factory that turns a callable into a
      \code{dsl::Predicate}.
\item \code{dsl::AST} (line 6723) contributes the static
      \code{make_leaf<Tag>} and \code{make_node<Tag>} templates, thin
      wrappers over the free functions \code{dsl::leaf} and
      \code{dsl::node}.
\item \code{dsl::Rewrite} (line 6922) contributes an \emph{instance}
      member \code{rewrite(const ASTNode&)} that applies the derived
      class's static \code{rules} member. The member is constrained with
      \code{requires requires { Derived::rules; }}, so it only exists
      for a derived class that actually declares \code{rules}.
\item \code{dsl::ExprTemplates} (line 7056) contributes no ordinary members
      at all. It contributes hidden friends: \code{operator+},
      \code{operator-}, \code{operator*} and \code{operator/}, binary and
      unary, defined inside the mixin so that argument-dependent lookup
      finds them only for the deriving type.
\item \code{dsl::CustomLiterals} (line 7265) contributes the static
      \code{parse_literal(std::string_view)}, constrained on
      \code{Derived::literals}.
\item \code{dsl::Memoization} (line 7290) contributes the instance member
      \code{clear_cache()}, which clears the derived class's
      \code{memo_cache} member.
\end{itemize}

Note the two kinds of member these produce. \code{Pipeline},
\code{Operators}, \code{AST} and \code{CustomLiterals} add \emph{static}
members, which are found through the class name and are therefore usable
without an instance. \code{Rewrite} and \code{Memoization} add
\emph{instance} members, because they act on per-object state: a rewrite
set, a cache. \code{ExprTemplates} adds neither; it adds operators.

\subsection{The Nineteen Marker Features}

The remaining nineteen tags have an empty \code{Mixin}: \code{EnumReflection}
(5897), \code{PatternMatch} (6503), \code{LazyFeature} (7303),
\code{Monadic} (7310), \code{ResultFeature} (7317),
\code{CombinatorParser} (7324), \code{TaskPipeline} (7331),
\code{SExpressions} (9091), \code{EqualitySaturation} (9097),
\code{Serialization} (9103), \code{AbstractIO} (9109),
\code{Visitors} (9115), \code{StaticRegularExpressions} (9121),
\code{Preprocessing} (9127), \code{ContextFreeGrammar} (9133),
\code{Graphs} (11405), \code{Terms} (11406), \code{TermRewriting} (11407)
and \code{GraphReduction} (11408).

These nineteen are not placeholders or stubs. Each corresponds to a
substantial, working subsystem that is usable on its own: \code{Maybe<T>},
\code{Result<T,E>}, the parser combinators, the PEG engine, the e-graph
saturator, the visitor compiler, the preprocessor, the Earley parser, the
graph and term machinery. What the marker does is let those subsystems be
\emph{named} in a feature list, so that \code{HasFeature<T, Visitors>} is a
meaningful question and so that the feature list reads as a declaration of
what kind of DSL this is.

\subsection{Why the Distinction Is Worth Making}

A reader who does not know about the two kinds will write
\code{MyDSL::match(...)} and get a compile error, or will assume that
naming \code{dsl::Visitors} gives them a visitor. The catalogue in
Section~\ref{sec:ch03-catalogue} therefore records, for every tag, whether
its mixin adds members and what those members are.

There is also a cost consideration. A marker mixin is an empty class, and
the empty base optimisation guarantees that it contributes zero bytes to the
layout of the deriving type. Seven injecting features do the same when their
members are all static. In neither case is there a vtable, a virtual
function, or any runtime overhead.

