  \draw[dsledge] (l4) -- (l5);
  \draw[dsledge] (l5) -- (l6);
\end{dslgraph}
\caption{The six layers of \DSLtk{}. Each layer uses only the layers beneath
it; nothing points downwards. The embedded enum-reflection library is not
shown, since it is a leaf library that layer 1 does not itself depend on.}
\label{fig:ch03-layers}
\end{figure}

Layer 1, the core value types, contains the small, self-contained types the
rest of the library is written in terms of: \code{FixedString}, the
compile-time string carrier; \code{ASTNode}, the value-type tree;
\code{Predicate} and \code{PipeStage}, the two composition wrappers;
\code{BinExpr} and \code{UnaryExpr}, the expression-template nodes;
\code{Maybe<T>} and \code{Result<T,E>}, the two error-handling wrappers;
\code{Lazy<T>}; and \code{SExpr}, the S-expression value. Every one of these
can be used with no DSL class anywhere in sight.

Layer 2 is the composition layer: the CRTP base class, the feature tags and
the two mixins that inject members. This is the layer a DSL author touches.

Layer 3 is parsing, in three flavours: the backtracking parser combinators
over \code{Parser<T>} and \code{ParsecInput} (lines 7664--7983), the PEG
engine built on \code{PEGDefinition} and \code{PEGMatcher} (8123--9085), and
the Earley-style context-free grammar machinery (10539--10793). All three
produce or consume the same \code{ASTNode} value type from layer 1.

Layer 4 is saturation: the S-expression reader and printer, and the
e-graph engine with its rewriting, matching and extraction machinery. This
is the layer that turns a set of rewrite rules into an optimal term.

Layer 5 is the boundary layer -- everything that touches the outside world
or crosses a representation boundary. Serialization protocols, abstract byte
I/O, schema-derived visitors, compile-time byte regular expressions and
binary streams, and the preprocessor with its optional Lua hook.

Layer 6 is graphs and terms: \code{DirectedGraph} with its algorithms and
DOT output, and the first-order term machinery with unification and bounded
reduction.

The layering is not merely decorative. It explains, for instance, why the
e-graph engine can extract an \code{ASTNode}: because layer 4 sits directly
on top of layer 1's value type. And it explains why the parser subsystems do
not know about the e-graph engine: layer 3 does not depend on layer 4.

\section{Everything Is a Namespace-Scope Free Function}

One convention runs through the whole library and deserves to be stated on
its own: essentially every public entry point is a free function at
namespace scope in \code{dsl}. The \code{leaf}, \code{node}, \code{rule},
\code{rewrite_set}, \code{match}, \code{when}, \code{otherwise},
\code{pattern}, \code{lit}, \code{literal_set}, \code{predicate},
\code{pipe}, \code{memoize}, \code{parser}, \code{ch}, \code{satisfy},
\code{optional}, \code{try_parse}, \code{labeled}, \code{run_parser},
\code{create_peg_definition}, \code{derive_peg_definition},
\code{peg_new_matcher}, \code{peg_seq}, \code{peg_choice},
\code{ast_schema}, \code{on}, \code{compile_visitor},
\code{make_cfg}, \code{sexpr_grammar}, \code{sexpr_rules} and
\code{parse_sexpr} are all free functions.

The consequences are practical. A facility can be used without defining a
DSL at all: \code{dsl::node<"add">(dsl::leaf<"num">(1),
dsl::leaf<"num">(2))} needs no base class, no inheritance, and no
configuration. And the mixins, where they exist at all, are thin
convenience wrappers that forward to the free function. \code{AST}'s
\code{make_leaf} is literally \code{return dsl::leaf<Tag>(...)}.

Listing~\ref{lst:ch03-free} shows this in practice: the same tree built
twice, once through the free function and once through the DSL's own static
members.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The free-function convention. Both routes produce the same tree; neither requires an instance of anything.},label={lst:ch03-free}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>

// A DSL built from *injecting* features: the mixins add real members.
struct Calc : dsl::DSL<Calc, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::AST> {};

// A DSL built from *marker* features: the mixins are empty.
struct Tagged : dsl::DSL<Tagged, dsl::PatternMatch, dsl::Visitors> {};

int main () {
  // The free function works with no DSL in sight at all.
  auto loose = dsl::node<"add"> (dsl::leaf<"num"> (1), dsl::leaf<"num"> (2));

  // The same tree, built through the DSL's own static members.
  auto owned = Calc::make_node<"add"> (Calc::make_leaf<"num"> (1),
                                       Calc::make_leaf<"num"> (2));

  std::cout << loose.dump () << "\n";
  std::cout << owned.dump () << "\n";
  std::cout << "sizeof(Calc)   = " << sizeof (Calc) << "\n";
  std::cout << "sizeof(Tagged) = " << sizeof (Tagged) << "\n";
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output is

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch03-free}. Both DSL types are one byte, because every mixin is empty and the empty base optimisation applies.},label={lst:ch03-free-out}]
(add (num 1) (num 2))
(add (num 1) (num 2))
sizeof(Calc)   = 1
sizeof(Tagged) = 1
\end{lstlisting}

The two \code{sizeof} lines are the point. A DSL that names three features
occupies exactly as much memory as one that names none, because every mixin
is an empty base class and the compiler is free to overlap it with anything
else. The features are a compile-time device and nothing more.

\subsection{How a Name Is Resolved}

The procedure below describes how a call site finds its
implementation. It is worth writing down because the answer is not always
the one a reader expects.

\begin{pseudocode}[Resolving a \code{dsl::} call site]
\textbf{function} resolve (name)\\
\quad \textbf{if} name is a member of the derived class then\\
\qquad \textbf{return} that member\\
\quad \textbf{if} name is a free function in \code{dsl} then\\
\qquad \textbf{return} that function\\
\quad \textbf{if} name is an operator then\\
\qquad \textbf{return} the hidden friend found by argument-dependent lookup\\
\qquad \qquad (only if an associated class is the deriving type)\\
\quad \textbf{otherwise}\\
\qquad \textbf{report} \emph{no such facility}
\end{pseudocode}

The third branch is the subtle one. Operators injected by
\code{dsl::ExprTemplates} are hidden friends, so they are found only when one
of the arguments is the deriving type or a class associated with it. That is
what keeps \code{operator+} from being ambiguous with every other
\code{operator+} in the program, and it is why the mixin's operators apply
to \code{Scalar} but not to \code{int}.

