\section{Consequences of the Single-Header Form}

Before leaving the physical structure, it is worth drawing out three
consequences of the library's packaging that recur throughout the book.

\paragraph{There is one translation unit, so the one-definition rule is
never at risk.}
Every function in \code{DSLtk.hpp} that is not a template is marked
\code{inline}, and every class template is instantiated once per program
rather than once per translation unit. A program that includes the header
in fifty translation units has fifty copies of the template definitions,
but exactly one copy of each inline function, and the linker discards the
duplicates. There is no ODR violation to worry about, and no need for
export macros, visibility attributes, or \code{#pragma once} discipline
beyond what the header already provides.

\paragraph{Compile time is paid per translation unit.}
This is the cost of the arrangement. Every translation unit that includes
\code{DSLtk.hpp} parses all 12,024 lines of it, and instantiates every
template it uses. The library's own examples are small enough that the
difference is imperceptible, but a large project that includes the header
widely will feel it. The mitigation is the three-tier wrapper arrangement
of Chapter~\ref{ch:getting-started}: include the toolkit in exactly one
wrapper header, and include only that wrapper elsewhere.

\paragraph{Everything is visible.}
There is no private implementation detail, because there is no separate
implementation. The embedded \code{magic_enum} block, the parser
combinators, the e-graph engine and the graph machinery are all in the same
file, in the same namespace, and are all equally accessible to the user.
This is a feature for a toolkit -- a DSL author can reach into the parser
combinators and use them directly -- and a hazard for anyone who wants to
hide implementation details. It also means that a name collision between
the library and user code is possible, which is why the wrapper idiom
exists.

\section{Auditing the Catalogue}

The catalogue in Section~\ref{sec:ch03-catalogue} is not a summary of the
documentation; it is a transcription of the source. Every tag was located
by searching the header for its declaration, and every mixin was read to
determine whether it was empty.

Two observations fall out of that exercise, and both are worth stating
because they are not visible from the documentation alone.

The first is that the shipped mixins are almost all thin. The seven that
contribute members either forward to a free function -- \code{AST}'s
\code{make_leaf} is \code{return dsl::leaf<Tag>(...)} -- or perform one
narrow operation on the deriving class's members. \code{Rewrite}'s
\code{rewrite} is a single call to \code{Derived::rules.apply(tree)}.
\code{CustomLiterals}' \code{parse_literal} is a single call to
\code{Derived::literals.parse(input)}. The mixins are adapters, not
implementations, and this is why they are cheap to instantiate.

The second is that the classification into injecting and marker features is
not a matter of degree. Of the twenty-six tags, nineteen have a mixin whose
body is empty or contains only comments, and seven have one that declares
members. There is no third category, and no tag that contributes
something in between.

\section{The D Port's Different Composition Mechanism}

The D implementation offers the same twenty-six feature tags and the same
library surface, but it composes them by a different mechanism, and the
contrast is instructive. In C++ a feature list becomes a list of base
classes. In D it becomes a mixin template applied to the enclosing struct.

Listing~\ref{lst:ch03-dmixin} shows the D port's \code{DSL!} mixin template
in full. It is the direct counterpart of \code{dsl::DSL<Derived,
Features...>}.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={The D port's composition mechanism, from \code{DSLtk.d} lines 92--121. Members are mixed into the enclosing aggregate; there is no base class.},label={lst:ch03-dmixin}]
/// Compose features in the enclosing aggregate: mixin DSL!(Pipeline, AST).
mixin template DSL(Features...)
{
    import std.meta : AliasSeq, NoDuplicates, staticIndexOf;
    import DSLtk;
    alias DSLFeatures = AliasSeq!Features;
    static assert(Features.length == NoDuplicates!Features.length,
                  "DSL features must be unique");
    static if (staticIndexOf!(Pipeline, Features) >= 0)
        static auto wrap(T)(T value) { return value; }
    static if (staticIndexOf!(Operators, Features) >= 0)
        static auto make_pred(F)(F fn) { return DSLtk.predicate(fn); }
    static if (staticIndexOf!(AST, Features) >= 0)
    {
        static auto make_leaf(string tag, T)(T value) { return DSLtk.leaf!tag(value); }
        static auto make_node(string tag, C...)(C children) { return DSLtk.node!tag(children); }
    }
    static if (staticIndexOf!(Rewrite, Features) >= 0)
        ASTNode rewrite(ASTNode tree) { return rules.apply(tree); }
    static if (staticIndexOf!(CustomLiterals, Features) >= 0)
        static real parse_literal(string input) { return literals.parse(input); }
    static if (staticIndexOf!(Memoization, Features) >= 0)
        void clear_cache() { memo_cache = null; }
    static if (staticIndexOf!(ExprTemplates, Features) >= 0)
        mixin ExpressionOperators;
    static foreach (Feature; Features)
    {
        static if (__traits(hasMember, Feature, "Mixin")) mixin Feature.Mixin;
    }
}
\end{lstlisting}

