Two differences deserve emphasis. The first is that the D port has no base
class at all. \code{struct BasicDSL { mixin DSL!(Pipeline, Operators); }}
produces a struct whose members are the mixin's members and nothing else;
there is no \code{BasicDSL} base, no protected \code{self()}, and no
\code{static_cast} dance to get back to the deriving type. The cost is that
D cannot express the C++ version's \code{static_cast<Derived&>(*this)}
pattern, so the D mixins simply call \code{rules}, \code{literals} and
\code{memo_cache} unqualified, relying on D's lexical scoping to find the
enclosing struct's members.

The second is the detection mechanism. C++ detects a feature by asking
whether a particular base class is present, which is exact and free.
D cannot ask that question of a mixin, so it records the feature list in an
alias \code{DSLFeatures} and searches it with \code{staticIndexOf}. The
result is a boolean template rather than a concept, and it answers the same
question.

\section{Edge Cases and Limits}

\paragraph{The marker features really do nothing.}
Nineteen of the twenty-six tags have an empty \code{Mixin}. Naming
\code{dsl::Visitors}, \code{dsl::Preprocessing}, \code{dsl::Graphs} or
\code{dsl::EqualitySaturation} in a feature list adds no members and no
behaviour. If you are expecting \code{MyDSL::compile_visitor(...)} to
appear because you named \code{dsl::Visitors}, it will not: you must call
\code{dsl::compile_visitor} yourself.

\paragraph{The documentation of \code{PatternMatch} is stale.}
The comment above the tag at line 6492 says that the feature exposes
\code{dsl::match}, \code{dsl::when} and \code{dsl::otherwise} as static
methods on the derived class. The mixin body contradicts this, and the
shipped code follows the body. Treat the free functions as the real API.

\paragraph{A feature tag must have a nested \code{Mixin}.}
\code{dsl::DSL<Derived, Features...>} expands to
\code{Features::template Mixin<Derived>...}, so a tag without a
\code{Mixin} template is a hard error at the point of instantiation:
\emph{``no class template named 'Mixin' in 'struct NoMixin'''}. This is the
one requirement the feature-tag protocol imposes, and the D port mirrors it
by checking \code{__traits(hasMember, Feature, "Mixin")}.

\paragraph{Features must be unique.}
Repeating a tag produces the error \emph{``duplicate base type''}. The D
port is friendlier here: it fails a \code{static assert} with a message that
names the problem.

\paragraph{There is no ordering guarantee between injecting features.}
Two injecting features that contributed a member of the same name would
produce an ambiguous lookup rather than a defined override. The shipped
features do not collide, but a custom feature that injects, say, a
\code{wrap} of its own will conflict with \code{dsl::Pipeline}'s, and the
error you get will be an ambiguity in name lookup, not a diagnostic about
features.

\paragraph{The feature list is not extensible at runtime.}
Features are a compile-time pack. There is no way to add a feature to a DSL
after the type has been defined, and no way to select features by a runtime
flag. This is a feature rather than a limitation, but it does mean that a
DSL whose feature set must vary at runtime has to be a template over the
feature pack rather than a single type.

\paragraph{Names inside the \code{dsl} namespace are not reserved by the
library.}
Because the convention is that every public entry point is a free function
in \code{dsl}, a user who writes \code{using namespace dsl;} and then
defines their own \code{dsl::leaf} or \code{dsl::match} will collide. The
three-tier wrapper idiom of Chapter~\ref{ch:getting-started} exists partly
to keep user code out of the \code{dsl} namespace.

\section{What Comes Next}

You now have a map: six layers, twenty-six feature tags of two distinct
kinds, one convention for public names, and one mechanism for composition.
Chapter~\ref{ch:crtp-foundation} opens layer 2 and takes apart the
twenty-line definition of \code{dsl::DSL<Derived, Features...>}, explains
why the mixins are inherited separately rather than gathered into one
aggregate, and shows what the CRTP pattern buys and what it costs.
